# Near-unit triangles in every large global minimizer

**Date:** 2026-10-11

**State base:** `ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`

**State version:** `2026-10-10.audit.234`

**Working predecessor:** `22488579`

**Target:** whole problem, with one scoped canonical geometric dependency

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

This theorem applies to **every** global unit-separated diameter minimizer.
No contact-minimal, stress-selected, rhombic-core, full-core, inactive-container,
or lattice-source normal form is imposed. Contact-isolated points count as
source points throughout. The new result does not claim any exact unit triangle.

## 1. Exact statement and global implication

Let `A` be any global minimizer of cardinality `n` and diameter `D`.

* If `D>=30000`, three actual source labels form a triangle all of whose
  sides belong to `[1,1+1450/D]`. Its largest squared side minus one is
  strictly less than `3000/D`.
* If `D>=60000`, `A` contains at least `ceil(n/144)` pairwise vertex-disjoint
  triangles of actual source labels whose sides belong to `[1,1+2900/D]`.
  For each of them, its largest squared side minus one is less than `6000/D`.

The constants are deliberately conservative. The companion global clearance
formulation therefore gives

\[
\boxed{\eta_n<3000/D(n)\quad\text{when }D(n)\ge30000.}     \tag{1}
\]

The exact implication chain is

```text
global lattice comparison + one coupled Delaunay crop
 -> quadratically many acute triangles of original source labels
 -> O(1/D) triangle clearance in every global minimizer
 -> no optimal clearance plateau extending beyond 3000/D
```

A plateau of any positive length below that bound remains possible under this
argument. Thus (1) is a global quantitative reduction, not Level I closure.

## 2. Dependencies and exact scope

The sole nontrivial geometric input is the canonical
`C-COUPLED-DELAUNAY-CONVEX-CONTAINER-TRANSFER`, promoted by Audit.108:
[`independent audit`](../audits/target_c_coupled_delaunay_container_transfer_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).
Its manifest scope is arbitrary finite unit-separated sources and arbitrary
convex polygon crops, so it applies to the full hull of any noncollinear global
minimizer. It is not restricted to canonical C-II survivors.

We use its proved completion, degree bound, triangle accounting, obtuse-pair
charge, and acute small-excess side estimate, not a source-contact or capacity
interpretation. Oler's finite inequality and the isodiametric and perimeter
bounds have the normalization recorded in
[`shared theory, Section 21.1`](../theory/03-oler-shell.md).
The triangular-lattice averaging comparison is reproved below.

## 3. The global population lower bound

Put `a0=sqrt(3)/2` and `Delta=pi/(2sqrt(3))`. A unit triangular lattice has
covolume `a0`. Average its number of sites in the **open** disk of radius
`D/2` over one fundamental cell of translations. The average is

\[
\frac{\pi D^2/4}{a_0}=\Delta D^2.
\]

If `n<=Delta D^2`, some translated lattice has at least `n` sites in that open
disk. Choose `n` of them. This finite unit-separated set has diameter strictly
less than `D`, contradicting global optimality. Therefore

\[
\boxed{n>\Delta D^2>\frac67D^2.}                          \tag{2}
\]

The last inequality uses only `pi>3` and `sqrt(3)<7/4`. This argument uses the
global hypothesis, independently of any contact graph or stress.

For `D>=30000` the source is noncollinear: a collinear unit-separated source
has `n<=D+1`, inconsistent with (2). Its full convex hull `K` is therefore a
compact convex polygon with nonempty interior.

## 4. Coupled completion and global linear defect budget

Use the single unit-separated closed-unit-covering completion `P` and its
Delaunay triangulation from Audit.108, with crop `C=K`. Write

\[
Q=\operatorname{area}(K),\quad p=\operatorname{per}(K),\quad
S=Q/a_0+p/2+1-n,
\]

and let `B` be the completion population inside `K` at distance at most two
from its boundary. Let `Sigma` be the sum of the nonnegative triangle area
excesses `e_T=area(T)-sqrt(3)/4` over all contained Delaunay triangles.

The isodiametric bound `Q<=pi D^2/4`, perimeter bound `p<=pi D`, and (2) give

\[
0\le S<\frac\pi2D+1<2D\qquad(D\ge3).
\]

Audit.108 gives

\[
\begin{split}
|(P\setminus A)\cap K|&\le\lfloor S\rfloor,\\
B&\le20p/\pi+25\le20D+25\le21D\quad(D\ge25),\\
0\le\Sigma&\le a_0S+7\sqrt3 B\\
&<\left(140\sqrt3+\frac{\pi\sqrt3}{4}\right)D
  +\left(175\sqrt3+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)\\
&<247D+308\le248D\quad(D\ge308).
\end{split}                                               \tag{3}
\]

The numerical inequalities use `sqrt(3)<7/4` and `pi<22/7`. They do not
assume a sharper minimizer shape or a bound on the isolate population.

## 5. Remove auxiliary and bad triangles, retaining quadratic source mass

Let `T` denote the number of contained Delaunay triangles. The canonical
Euler accounting in Audit.108 gives

\[
T\ge2n-28B.                                               \tag{4}
\]

Here the `n` labels are all original source points; the completion was not
substituted for the source. Its Delaunay degree bound is 24. Therefore at
most `24B` contained triangles touch the boundary strip, and at most `24S`
contained triangles touch an auxiliary completion vertex in `K`.

The canonical obtuse-triangle pair charge applies to triangles all of whose
vertices are deeper than two. It bounds their right/obtuse population by
`4Sigma/delta`, where `delta=(2-sqrt(3))/4>1/16`.

After deleting those three populations, at least

\[
\begin{split}
N&\ge T-24B-24S-4\Sigma/\delta\\
&\ge2n-52B-24S-64\Sigma\\
&>2n-17012D                                                \tag{5}
\end{split}
\]

acute contained triangles remain whose three vertices are **original source
labels**. If a boundary triangle is also auxiliary or obtuse, this deletion
only overcounts losses, which is harmless for the lower bound.

For `D>=30000`, (2) implies `n>17012D`, so `N>n`. This is the decisive
source-population check: `O(D)` completion and exceptional labels cannot
consume the quadratic original source triangle population.

## 6. One almost-unit source triangle and the plateau bound

The sum of the retained triangles' excesses is at most `Sigma`. One of them
therefore has

\[
e_T\le\Sigma/N<248D/n<\frac{868}{3D}<\frac{290}{D}.
\]

For `D>=30000` this is less than `1/100`. The canonical acute small-excess
side estimate gives, for every one of its three sides,

\[
1\le L\le1+5e_T<1+1450/D.
\]

Hence

\[
\max L^2-1
<\frac{2900}{D}+\frac{2102500}{D^2}<\frac{3000}{D},        \tag{6}
\]

where the last inequality holds for `D>=30000`. This proves the first
conclusion. Since it holds for every global minimizer, maximizing its
triangle clearance over the entire compact optimizer set proves (1).
It follows that `F_n(s)>F_n(0)` for `s>=3000/D(n)` in that range.
The positive interval below this threshold is not excluded.

## 7. A macroscopic disjoint family of almost-unit source triangles

If `Sigma>0`, at least half of the `N` retained triangles have
`e_T<=2Sigma/N<580/D`. If `Sigma=0`, all `N` do. For `D>=60000` these
excesses are below `1/100`, so their sides are less than `1+2900/D` and
their largest squared side excess is less than

\[
5800/D+8410000/D^2<6000/D.
\]

There are at least `n/2` such triangles. A triangle has three vertices, and
each of those belongs to at most 24 triangles in the single coupled
Delaunay triangulation. Greedily select a triangle and remove every triangle
sharing one of its vertices. Each selection removes at most 72 candidates.
Thus one obtains at least `ceil(n/144)` mutually vertex-disjoint source
triangles with the stated side and clearance bounds.

No disjointness is inferred from independent charts or different completions.
All vertices and triangles come from the one actual full-source completion.

## 8. Checks, limitations, and next global obligation

`research/verify_global_source_near_triangle_budget_2026_10_11.py` checks the
rational envelopes and positivity certificates for every real `D>=30000`,
using polynomial coefficients in `D-30000`; it checks the second family's
threshold at `60000`. It does not certify the geometric input, completion,
global optimality, exact unit contacts, or Level I. Those scope restrictions
are explicit. The analytic argument above carries the source-population and
global quantifiers.

In particular, none of the near-unit Delaunay edges is asserted to be an old
source contact. A positive `O(1/D)` clearance can persist in a triangle-free
source; taking a limit after rescaling the configuration does not create an
exact unit triangle at a finite cardinality. The companion parallel-contact
approximation supplies an exact warning against such an inference.

The remaining global obligation is to eliminate the optimal clearance
plateau **inside** `0<s<3000/D(n)` for all sufficiently large cardinalities.
Equivalently one must upgrade the bound on the maximum optimizer clearance
from (1) to `eta_n=0`. No canonical status is changed by this candidate.

**Promotion recommendation:** preserve unreviewed; the sole canonical crop
dependency has been checked at its full-source scope. Do not promote a Level I
conclusion.
